nsformer forward\"\n582\t      ],\n583\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n584\t        \"modular addition mod prime p, exhaustive p^2 input pairs (Power et al. algorithmic datasets)\",\n585\t        \"one-block transformer on modular addition (Nanda et al.)\"\n586\t      ],\n587\t      \"central_topic\": \"How network width (model size) relates to the onset delay of grokking on modular-addition tasks in small transformers, tested via a memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis with a width-by-train-fraction sweep, right-censored delay metrics, bootstrap slope CIs, and muP-style parametrization controls.\",\n588\t      \"comparators\": [\n589\t        \"circuit-efficiency explanation of grokking\",\n590\t        \"lazy-to-rich training dynamics\",\n591\t        \"weight-norm / loss-landscape (Omnigrok) account\",\n592\t        \"grokking and double descent unification\",\n593\t        \"memorization-capacity and memorization-dynamics literature\"\n594\t      ],\n595\t      \"core_methods\": [\n596\t        \"grokking delay measurement with right-censoring on modular addition\",\n597\t        \"width x train-fraction sweeps of small transformers with bootstrap CIs on log-delay slope\",\n598\t        \"memorization-capacity crossover hypothesis (t_mem vs t_gen, critical width vs train fraction)\",\n599\t        \"width-aware output scaling (muP-like) and hidden-layer W=m*V reparametrization to control effective learning rate\",\n600\t        \"weight decay / AdamW training\"\n601\t      ],\n602\t      \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n603\t        \"generic 'optimization', 'benchmarking', 'scientific method', 'paper writing' phrasing\",\n604\t        \"chronology/search-history queries (cycle order, idea numbering, combined score)\",\n605\t        \"queries phrased as 'effect of model size/width on grokking delay' or 'scaling of grokking with model size' (risk of surfacing the excluded prior paper; the starter literature plus c\n606\t\n607\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n608\t```\n609\t\n610\t## manuscript source excerpt\n611\t\n612\t```tex\n613\t\\documentclass{article}\n614\t\n615\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n616\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n617\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n618\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n619\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n620\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n621\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n622\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n623\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n624\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n625\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n626\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n627\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n628\t}\n629\t\n630\t \n631\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n632\t\n633\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n634\t\\usepackage{url}\n635\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n636\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n637\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n638\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n639\t\n640\t\\title{Does Grokking Delay Depend on Width? An Inconclusive, Censoring-Aware Slope Test on Modular Addition}\n641\t\\author{Anonymous authors}\n642\t\n643\t\\begin{document}\n644\t\\maketitle\n645\t\n646\t\\begin{abstract}\n647\tDelayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic is explained through memorization capacity, weight norm, circuit efficiency, or a lazy-to-rich transition, but its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement.\n648\tWe test one falsifiable prediction in a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW (weight decay $1.0$, full batch) on modular addition modulo $97$: that the delay between fitting the training pairs and generalizing to held-out pairs shrinks as width grows.\n649\tDelay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\n650\tThe measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\n651\tA single-seed reference trainer gives $-0.368$, inside the interval.\n652\tThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure; we state what it cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n653\t\\end{abstract}\n654\t\n655\t\\section{Introduction}\n656\t\\label{sec:intro}\n657\tIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n658\t\n659\tThis paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n660\t\n661\tThe outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n662\t\n663\t\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n664\t\n665\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n666\t\\centering\n667\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n668\t\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one probe interval and set to the step limit if either threshold is never reached. The slope of $\\ln$(delay) against $\\log_2$(width) is estimated over five widths and four seeds, with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval; the interval, not the point estimate, determines the verdict. In this study the interval straddles zero and the verdict is inconclusive.}\n669\t\\label{fig:overview}\n670\t\\end{figure}\n671\t\n672\t\\section{Related Work}\n673\t\\label{sec:related}\n674\t\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. Empirical studies have varied some of those knobs on modular arithmetic or related algorithmic tasks: \\citet{Doshi2023ToGO} study modular arithmetic with corrupted labels and examine how generalization and memorization coexist under regularization; \\citet{Thilak2022TheSM} associate grokking under adaptive optimizers with cyclic behavior of the last-layer norm; and \\citet{Prieto2025GrokkingAT} attribute the absence of grokking without regularization to numerical instability in the softmax. We do not re-derive these findings, and none of them has, as far as their stated scope and our non-exhaustive reading indicate, measured delay against width under a fixed protocol, which is the quantity examined here; the present study does not test the accounts against each other.\n675\t\n676\t\\paragraph{Model size, memorization, and double descent.} Test error can vary non-monotonically with model size and training time \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}; networks can fit random labels, so fitting the training set does not by itself imply generalization \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}; memorization of training data has been studied empirically during language-model training \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO}, and a method for estimating how much information a model stores about data points has been proposed and measured on language models \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}. These works motivate a capacity-based reading of the grokking delay, in which fitting time and generalization time depend differently on width. We use them only as motivation: the artifacts of this study do not record fitting time and generalization time separately.\n677\t\n678\t\\paragraph{Parametrization and optimizer scale.} The evaluated model contains width-dependent multipliers on hidden and output layers (Section~\\ref{sec:model}), motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The width-aware parametrization literature makes this precise: \\citet{Yang2021TensorPI} characterize a maximal-update parametrization in which hidden-layer features keep learning as width grows, and \\citet{Yang2022TensorPV} show that hyperparameters such as the learning rate then transfer across widths. Our multipliers resemble this construction in spirit, but we do not claim that they implement it, and we did not check hyperparameter transfer; the point for this study is only that a width-dependent multiplier can change how fast wider models move, and so can mask or create a width slope. The role of effective learning rates under normalization has been studied in reinforcement learning \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}, practical measures of dynamical richness have been proposed \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}, and decoupled weight decay, which we use, is introduced by \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. We do not measure the effect of these multipliers in isolation.\n679\t\n680\t\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n681\t\n682\t\\section{Method}\n683\t\\label{sec:method}\n684\t\n685\t\\subsection{Problem setup and hypothesis}\n686\t\\label{sec:setup}\n687\t\\paragraph{Task.} Inputs are all $p^2=9{,}409$ ordered pairs $(a,b)$ with $a,b\\in\\{0,\\dots,96\\}$ and label $(a+b)\\bmod 97$. For each seed, a random $40\\%$ of pairs ($3{,}763$) forms the training split and the remaining $5{,}646$ pairs are held out; the evaluator, not the model, owns the split, the checkpoint schedule, and all metrics.\n688\t\n689\t\\paragraph{Delay.} Training runs for at most $T=10{,}000$ full-batch steps and is probed every $250$ steps, giving $40$ checkpoints at which accuracy is computed by the evaluator on all pairs. Let $t_{\\mathrm{fit}}$ be the first checkpoint at which training accuracy is at least $0.99$, and $t_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ the first at which held-out accuracy is at least $0.99$. The delay is\n690\t\\begin{equation}\n691\t\\Delta = \\begin{cases} \\max(t_{\\mathrm{gen}}-t_{\\mathrm{fit}},\\,250) & \\text{if both thresholds are reached,}\\\\ 10{,}000 & \\text{otherwise (censored).}\\end{cases}\n692\t\\end{equation}\n693\tA run is censored if \\emph{either} threshold is never reached. Censored runs keep the cap value and are never dropped or reweighted. The floor of one probe interval ($250$ steps) also applies when held-out accuracy crosses $0.99$ no later than training accuracy does. Both the floor and the cap therefore bound $\\ln\\Delta$ to $[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, a ratio of $40$.\n694\t\n695\t\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed; \\citealp{Cameron2015APG}). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n696\t\n697\t\\paragraph{Caveat on few clusters.} The cluster-robust inference literature documents that inference with a small number of clusters can be unreliable, and that refinements to the basic cluster bootstrap, such as the wild cluster bootstrap-$t$, can improve coverage \\citep{Cameron2008BootstrapBasedIF,Cameron2015APG}. We use the plain percentile interval with four clusters as pre-declared, make no claim of nominal coverage, and treat the interval as a coarse summary of seed-to-seed variability rather than an exact confidence statement.\n698\t\n699\t\\paragraph{What the interval does and does not capture.} Each seed fixes both the train/held-out split and the weight initialization, and the design has exactly one run per seed and width. Resampling seeds therefore propagates split and initialization variability jointly, and a seed is the unit of replication; run-to-run nondeterminism within a seed is not separated from it, and cannot be, since no seed is repeated at a width in the sealed design. Because the design is balanced, the pooled OLS slope equals the slope fitted to the width-wise means of $\\ln\\Delta$, and the bootstrap interval is the percentile range of that quantity over seed resamples.\n700\t\n701\t\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n702\t\\label{sec:model}\n703\tThe evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $97$ residues plus a separator token ``$=$'' appended as the third input position, and a learned positional embedding. Attention is non-causal and only the representation at the final position is read out; the implementation computes queries, residual stream, and MLP for that position only, which by inspection of the one-layer architecture gives the same outputs as processing all positions. Writing $z_i=E[x_i]+P_i$ for the embedded tokens $x_1,x_2,x_3{=}$``$=$'', the evaluated model computes\n704\t\\begin{equation}\n705\th=z_3+W_O\\,\\mathrm{Attn}(z_1,z_2,z_3),\\qquad h'=h+W_2\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}(W_1h+b_1)+b_2,\\qquad \\text{logits}=m_d\\,W_U\\,h',\n706\t\\end{equation}\n707\twith $W_\\ast=m_dV_\\ast$ for the attention and MLP matrices (Modification 3 below; $m_d=1$ recovers the plain parametrization) and $W_U$ the unembedding. The parameter count is $12d^2+(2p+9)d$, from $6{,}320$ at $d=16$ to $838{,}400$ at $d=256$, within the $10^6$ budget (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-model}).\n708\t\n709\tRelative to the evaluator's stock model, the configuration has three modifications, all visible in the source (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_model/program.py}):\n710\t\\begin{enumerate}\n711\t\\item \\emph{Smaller initial weights.} All hidden and output weight matrices are multiplied by $0.5$ at initialization.\n712\t\\item \\emph{Width-aware output scaling.} The unembedding weights are additionally halved at initialization, and the logits are multiplied by $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$.\n713\t\\item \\emph{Hidden-layer reparametrization.} Each hidden matrix is written $W=m_d V$ with the same multiplier $m_d$, and $V$ is initialized as $W_{\\mathrm{init}}/m_d$ so that the function at initialization is unchanged. The optimizer updates $V$, and decoupled weight decay acts on $V$. The code comments state the intent: under Adam-type updates $V$ moves at about the learning rate per step, so $W$ moves at about $m_d$ times the learning rate, which is meant to compensate for width in the effective step size.\n714\t\\end{enumerate}\n715\tThe multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n716\t\n717\t\\subsection{Reference trainer, controls, and validity}\n718\t\\label{sec:controls}\n719\t\\paragraph{Reference trainer.} The evaluator also trains its own stock transformer with the same optimizer settings and no modifications (no initialization shrinkage, no multipliers, all positions processed), using the first final seed ($201$) at each of the five widths. The OLS slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ over these five single-seed delays is the reference slope. It has no interval.\n720\t\n721\t\\paragraph{Controls.} Two control runs at width $64$ and seed $201$ check that the measurement does not manufacture generalization: one trains on training labels that have been permuted (at least $90\\%$ of labels changed) and one performs zero optimizer steps. A control fails if its held-out accuracy ever exceeds $0.10$; chance level for $97$ classes is about $0.010$.\n722\t\n723\t\\paragraph{Validity.} The evaluator marks the final evaluation valid (\\texttt{qualified}, status \\texttt{completed}) when, according to its source, all episodes completed, the compute and call budgets were respected, the controls passed, and at least one run fit the training data to $0.99$. Only these three status fields are retained in the artifacts; per-condition diagnostics beyond them are not, so we treat any finer breakdown as unknown. Diagnostics reported by the evaluated model itself are untrusted and not used: every accuracy, delay, control, and statistic below is computed by the evaluator from returned predictions.\n724\t\n725\t\\subsection{Scope of the retained evidence}\n726\t\\label{sec:scope}\n727\tThe retained final artifacts consist of the aggregate metrics listed in Section~\\ref{sec:results} and one record per seed (mean validation-accuracy score and wall-clock time). \\emph{Per-width and per-seed delays, fitting and generalization steps, and per-width censoring flags are not retained}, so we cannot show a delay-versus-width plot, and we do not infer one. Separately, a development evaluation on a different data plane (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, widths $32,64,128$, $6{,}000$ steps, two seeds) was used when configuring the evaluated model; its results are reported only as descriptive context in Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev} and never mixed with the sealed result.\n728\t\n729\t\\section{Results}\n730\t\\label{sec:results}\n731\t\n732\t\\subsection{Main result: the slope test is inconclusive}\n733\tThe sealed evaluation completed with status \\texttt{qualified}. Its aggregate metrics are listed in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics}.\n734\t\n735\tThe slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neither supported nor refuted. A point estimate of $-0.020$ corresponds to a fitted multiplicative change of $0.980$ in delay per doubling of width, and the interval to factors between $0.557$ and $1.425$; over the full $16\\to256$ range the fitted factors are $0.924$ with interval $[0.096, 4.12]$.\n736\t\n737\tThat is, the data are compatible with a delay roughly $10$ times shorter at width $256$ than at $16$, with no change, and with a delay about $4$ times longer.\n738\t\n739\t\\begin{table}[t]\n740\t\\centering\n741\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation: aggregate metrics (four seeds $\\times$ five widths $=20$ runs, plus two controls). ``Score'' is the mean over seeds and widths of the area under the held-out-accuracy curve over the $40$ checkpoints; it does not use the $0.99$ threshold.}\n742\t\\label{tab:metrics}\n743\t\\small\n744\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n745\t\\toprule\n746\tQuantity & Value \\\\\n747\t\\midrule\n748\tSlope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ (point) & $-0.020$ \\\\\n749\t95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval & $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$ \\\\\n750\tVerdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\n751\tReference-trainer slope (seed 201 only, no interval) & $-0.368$ \\\\\n752\tCensored fraction of the 20 runs & $0.40$ ($8$ runs) \\\\\n753\tControl maximum held-out accuracy (ceiling $0.10$) & $0.012$ \\\\\n754\tMissing episodes & $0$ \\\\\n755\tScore (mean held-out-accuracy AUC) & $0.729$ \\\\\n756\t\\bottomrule\n757\t\\end{tabular}\n758\t\\end{table}\n759\t\n760\t\n761\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n762\t\\centering\n763\t\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.pdf}\n764\t\\caption{The estimated slope and its 95\\% interval against the three decision regions. The interval straddles zero, so the verdict is inconclusive. The single-seed reference slope lies inside the interval. Dotted lines mark the largest slope magnitude attainable ($1.107$) when every width-wise mean delay is confined to $[250, 10{,}000]$.}\n765\t\\label{fig:slope}\n766\t\\end{figure}\n767\t\n768\t\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n769\t\\label{sec:resolution}\n770\tAn inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Table~\\ref{tab:resolution}), limit that resolution.\n771\t\n772\t\n773\t\\emph{Bounded dynamic range.} In this balanced design the slope is a fixed linear contrast of the width-wise mean log-delays $m_d=\\tfrac14\\sum_s\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$:\n774\t\\begin{equation}\n775\t\\beta=\\sum_{d} w_d\\,m_d,\\qquad (w_{16},\\dots,w_{256})=(-0.2,\\,-0.1,\\,0,\\,0.1,\\,0.2),\n776\t\\end{equation}\n777\tthat is, $\\beta=0.2\\,(m_{256}-m_{16})+0.1\\,(m_{128}-m_{32})$; the middle width carries no weight, and the verdict depends only on how the two widest models compare with the two narrowest. Because every $m_d\\in[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, $|\\beta|\\le 0.3\\ln 40=1.107$, attained by a step profile; a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at $d=16$ to the floor at $d=256$ would give $0.922$. The interval has width $0.939$ and covers $42\\%$ of the attainable range $[-1.107,1.107]$. Its lower arm extends $0.565$ below the point estimate and its upper arm $0.374$ above.\n778\t\n779\t\\emph{Distance to a supported verdict.} Keeping the upper arm of the interval unchanged, a verdict of supported would have required a point estimate below $-0.374$. The reference slope ($-0.368$) is slightly above that value; with the same upper arm it would give an upper endpoint of $+0.006$, still straddling zero. This is arithmetic on the observed spread, not a forecast: the spread of a different point estimate would differ.\n780\t\n781\t\\emph{A coarse bootstrap.} With four seeds there are only $\\binom{7}{4}=35$ distinct multisets of seeds to resample, and a fraction $4/4^4=1/64$ of draws consist of a single seed repeated four times. The $2{,}000$ resamples therefore estimate a discrete distribution with at most $35$ support points; the percentile endpoints $-0.585$ and $0.354$ should be read as coarse, and the coverage of an interval built from four clusters is not guaranteed to be nominal.\n782\t\n783\t\\begin{table}[t]\n784\t\\centering\n785\t\\caption{Quantities derived from the protocol constants and the reported interval (not additional measurements).}\n786\t\\label{tab:resolution}\n787\t\\small\n788\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n789\t\\toprule\n790\tQuantity & Value \\\\\n791\t\\midrule\n792\tDelay floor / cap (steps) & $250$ / $10{,}000$ \\\\\n793\tLargest attainable $|\\text{slope}|$ (step profile) & $1.107$ \\\\\n794\tSlope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile & $0.922$ \\\\\n795\tInterval width & $0.939$ \\\\\n796\tInterval width as fraction of $[-1.107, 1.107]$ & $0.42$ \\\\\n797\tInterval arms below / above the point estimate & $0.565$ / $0.374$ \\\\\n798\tPer-doubling delay factor: point [interval] & $0.980$ [$0.557$, $1.425$] \\\\\n799\t$16\\to256$ delay factor: point [interval] & $0.924$ [$0.096$, $4.12$] \\\\\n800\t$16\\to256$ delay factor at the reference slope & $0.230$ \\\\\n801\tDistinct bootstrap seed multisets & $35$ \\\\\n802\t\\bottomrule\n803\t\\end{tabular}\n804\t\\end{table}\n805\t\n806\t\\subsection{Censoring}\n807\tForty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n808\t\n809\t\n810\t\\subsection{Seed-level variability}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d81007c6-4501-439f-bb6b-807dec8e1fee","uuid":"1e75410a-ddf7-4104-af1b-d9cc2754c078","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:47:35.339Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.md","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T14:47:30.400516+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261111_140816-paper_evidence_audit_round_2-qd7e3hvk/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims_groundi